---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/utils';
import Animations from '../../components/Animations.vue';
import CommentsSection from '../../components/CommentsSection.vue';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { lang: 'en' } },
    { params: { lang: 'es' } },
    { params: { lang: 'pt' } },
  ];
}

const { lang } = Astro.params;
const t = useTranslations(lang);

const content = {
  en: {
    title: "Cloud Run and Serverless Containers — Architecture, GPUs, and Trade-offs",
    date: "October 3, 2026",
    subtitle: "A developer diary entry by Jill",
    back: 'Back to Home',
    intro: "Understanding Google Cloud Run: serverless container deployment, auto-scaling to zero, and the addition of GPU acceleration.",
    introDesc: "A technical overview covering container configuration, cold starts vs provisioned concurrency, and GPU workloads.",
    introExtra: [
      "Launched in 2019 based on the open-source Knative framework.",
      "Combines container flexibility with scale-to-zero serverless economics."
    ],
    section1: {
      heading: "1. What Is Cloud Run & How Does It Work?",
      paragraphs: [
        `Traditional **PaaS** *(if you're unfamiliar with this concept, check out my <a href="/en/blog-cloud" class="inline-blog-link">entry explaining SaaS, PaaS, and IaaS</a>)* like Heroku or App Engine required specific runtime configurations. Kubernetes (GKE) offers complete control but requires cluster management.`,
        "**Cloud Run** bridges this gap: you provide any standard Open Container Initiative (OCI) image (Docker container listening on an HTTP port), and Cloud Run manages infrastructure, TLS certificates, load balancing, and scaling.",
        "When no requests arrive, instances scale down to **0** (saving cost). When traffic spikes, Cloud Run spins up parallel container instances automatically."
      ]
    },
    section2: {
      heading: "2. Key Technical Features: Cold Starts vs Concurrency",
      paragraphs: [
        "**• Concurrency:** Unlike AWS Lambda (which handles 1 request per container instance by default), Cloud Run handles up to 1000 concurrent requests per container instance, drastically reducing instance requirements.",
        `**• Cold Starts:** When an instance starts from zero, initial latency increases. For production services and critical **APIs** *(see my <a href="/en/blog-apis" class="inline-blog-link">entry on why APIs are conversations</a>)*, setting \`--min-instances 1\` keeps a warm instance active.`,
        "**• GPU Support:** Cloud Run now supports **NVIDIA L4 GPUs**, allowing serverless hosting of open-weight LLMs (like Llama 3 or Gemma) without managing GKE clusters."
      ]
    },
    section3: {
      heading: "3. Deployment Command Example using Google Cloud CLI",
      paragraphs: [
        `Deploying a service to Cloud Run *(if you want to read about deployment pipelines and fears, check out my <a href="/en/blog-deployment" class="inline-blog-link">entry on deployments</a>)* requires only a built container image stored in Artifact Registry:`
      ],
      codeLabel: "Cloud Run CLI Deployment Command",
      code: `# Deploy a Node.js/Python container service with GPU acceleration
gcloud run deploy customer-api \\
  --image us-central1-docker.pkg.dev/jill-proj/apps/api:v1.2 \\
  --region us-central1 \\
  --platform managed \\
  --allow-unauthenticated \\
  --concurrency 80 \\
  --cpu 2 \\
  --memory 4Gi \\
  --min-instances 1`
    },
    section4: {
      heading: "4. Practical Use Cases & Architecture Trade-offs",
      paragraphs: [
        `**Best Use Cases:** REST/GraphQL APIs, microservices, **Webhooks** *(if you want to know how webhooks differ from traditional APIs, read my <a href="/en/blog-webhooks" class="inline-blog-link">entry on Webhooks</a>)*, background job processing, and containerized ML inference.`,
        "**Trade-offs:** Max request execution timeout is capped at 60 minutes for HTTP services. WebSocket connections work but require connection pinning considerations."
      ]
    }
  },
  es: {
    title: "Cloud Run y Contenedores Serverless — Arquitectura, GPUs y Consideraciones",
    date: "3 de Octubre, 2026",
    subtitle: "El diario de desarrollo de Jill",
    back: 'Volver al Inicio',
    intro: "Una mirada técnica a Cloud Run de Google Cloud: despliegue serverless de contenedores, escalado a cero y aceleración con GPUs.",
    introDesc: "Guía práctica sobre configuración de contenedores, tiempos de arranque en frío (cold start) y workloads de microservicios.",
    introExtra: [
      "Lanzado en 2019 sobre el estándar de código abierto Knative.",
      "Combina la flexibilidad de Docker con la economía serverless de pago por uso real."
    ],
    section1: {
      heading: "1. ¿Qué es Cloud Run y cómo funciona?",
      paragraphs: [
        `Los **PaaS** tradicionales *(si no estás familiarizado con este término, puedes consultar mi <a href="/es/blog-cloud" class="inline-blog-link">entrada donde explico la diferencia entre SaaS, PaaS e IaaS</a>)* exigían runtimes específicos, mientras que Kubernetes (GKE) ofrece control total pero requiere administrar clusters.`,
        "**Cloud Run** resuelve esto: empaquetas cualquier aplicación en una imagen de contenedor estándar (Docker que escuche peticiones en un puerto HTTP), y Cloud Run administra la infraestructura, balanceo de carga, certificados TLS y escalado.",
        "Si no hay peticiones, el servicio escala a **0 instancias** (ahorrando costos). Cuando llega tráfico, Cloud Run levanta contenedores automáticamente."
      ]
    },
    section2: {
      heading: "2. Características Técnicas: Concurrencia y Cold Starts",
      paragraphs: [
        "**• Concurrencia:** A diferencia de funciones serverless tradicionales que atienden 1 petición por instancia, Cloud Run puede procesar hasta 1000 peticiones concurrentes en un mismo contenedor.",
        `**• Cold Starts (Arranques en frío):** Cuando una instancia arranca desde cero, existe una latencia inicial. Para **APIs** *(revisa mi <a href="/es/blog-apis" class="inline-blog-link">diario donde explico que las APIs son conversaciones</a>)* críticas se puede configurar \`--min-instances 1\`.`,
        "**• Soporte de GPUs:** La integración con **GPUs NVIDIA L4** permite ejecutar modelos de IA en contenedores con modelo serverless sin gestionar clusters de Kubernetes."
      ]
    },
    section3: {
      heading: "3. Ejemplo de Comando de Despliegue en Cloud CLI",
      paragraphs: [
        `Desplegar un microservicio en Cloud Run *(si quieres leer sobre la evolución y miedos al hacer despliegues, consulta mi <a href="/es/blog-deployment" class="inline-blog-link">entrada sobre el deploy</a>)* requiere simplemente indicar la imagen alojada en Artifact Registry:`
      ],
      codeLabel: "Comando gcloud run deploy",
      code: `# Desplegar un contenedor Node.js/Python a Cloud Run
gcloud run deploy customer-api \\
  --image us-central1-docker.pkg.dev/jill-proj/apps/api:v1.2 \\
  --region us-central1 \\
  --platform managed \\
  --allow-unauthenticated \\
  --concurrency 80 \\
  --cpu 2 \\
  --memory 4Gi \\
  --min-instances 1`
    },
    section4: {
      heading: "4. Casos de Uso y Comparativa",
      paragraphs: [
        `**Casos ideales:** APIs REST/GraphQL, **Webhooks** *(si quieres saber qué son y cómo se diferencian de una API tradicional, consulta mi <a href="/es/blog-webhooks" class="inline-blog-link">entrada sobre Webhooks</a>)*, microservicios orientados a eventos e inferencia de modelos ML en contenedores.`,
        "**Consideraciones:** El tiempo límite de ejecución por petición HTTP es de máximo 60 minutos. Aunque soporta WebSockets, se debe considerar el timeout de conexiones prolongadas."
      ]
    }
  },
  pt: {
    title: "Cloud Run e Contêineres Serverless — Arquitetura, GPUs e Casos Práticos",
    date: "3 de Outubro, 2026",
    subtitle: "O diário de desenvolvimento de Jill",
    back: 'Voltar ao Início',
    intro: "Um resumo técnico do Cloud Run: implantação serverless de contêineres OCI, escalabilidade automática e GPUs.",
    introDesc: "Visão técnica sobre concorrência, tempos de inicialização (cold start) e microsserviços.",
    introExtra: [
      "Lançado em 2019 com base no projeto open-source Knative.",
      "Combina a flexibilidade do Docker com custos de escala a zero."
    ],
    section1: {
      heading: "1. O que é o Cloud Run?",
      paragraphs: [
        `O Cloud Run permite rodar qualquer contêiner Docker sob o modelo **PaaS** *(se não estiver familiarizado com PaaS, confira meu <a href="/pt/blog-cloud" class="inline-blog-link">post explicando SaaS, PaaS e IaaS</a>)* de forma serverless.`,
        "Se não houver tráfego, o serviço reduz para **0 instâncias**. Quando chegam requisições, ele escala instâncias em contêineres automaticamente."
      ]
    },
    section2: {
      heading: "2. Recursos Técnicos",
      paragraphs: [
        "**• Concorrência:** Suporta múltiplas requisições simultâneas por contêiner.",
        `**• Instâncias Mínimas:** \`--min-instances\` evita latência de cold start para **APIs** *(confira meu <a href="/pt/blog-apis" class="inline-blog-link">post sobre APIs</a>)*.`,
        "**• Suporte a GPU:** Suporte a **GPUs NVIDIA L4** para cargas de IA."
      ]
    },
    section3: {
      heading: "3. Exemplo de Comando CLI",
      paragraphs: [
        `Comando padrão para implantação de contêiner *(relembre os desafios de implantação no meu <a href="/pt/blog-deployment" class="inline-blog-link">post sobre deploy</a>)* no Cloud Run:`
      ],
      codeLabel: "Comando gcloud deploy",
      code: `gcloud run deploy customer-api \\
  --image us-central1-docker.pkg.dev/jill-proj/apps/api:v1.2 \\
  --region us-central1 \\
  --platform managed \\
  --allow-unauthenticated \\
  --concurrency 80 \\
  --cpu 2 \\
  --memory 4Gi \\
  --min-instances 1`
    },
    section4: {
      heading: "4. Casos de Uso e Limitações",
      paragraphs: [
        `**Casos de uso:** APIs REST, microsserviços e **Webhooks** *(se quiser saber sobre Webhooks, veja meu <a href="/pt/blog-webhooks" class="inline-blog-link">post de Webhooks</a>)*.`,
        "**Limitações:** Tempo máximo por requisição HTTP de até 60 minutos."
      ]
    }
  }
};

const c = content[lang] || content.es;
---

<Layout title={`${c.title} | Jill Portfolio`} lang={lang}>
  <Animations client:load />
  
  <div class="blog-post-page">
    <Nav activeSection="blog" lang={lang} />
    
    <main class="container">
      <article class="post-article">
        <a href={`/${lang}/#blog`} class="back-link">
          <span class="arrow">←</span> {c.back}
        </a>

        <header class="post-header">
          <div class="meta-badge">
            Cloud & Serverless Containers
          </div>
          <h1 class="post-title">{c.title}</h1>
          <p class="post-subtitle">{c.subtitle}</p>
          <div class="post-date">
            <span class="calendar-icon">📅</span> {c.date}
          </div>
        </header>

        <div class="post-card">
          <section class="intro-section">
            <div class="intro-bubble">
              <p class="intro-main">{c.intro}</p>
              <p class="intro-desc">{c.introDesc}</p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="extra-bullets">
              {c.introExtra.map(bullet => (
                <div class="bullet-item">
                  <span class="bullet-icon">•</span>
                  <span>{bullet}</span>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          </section>

          <hr class="divider" />

          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-heading">{c.section1.heading}</h2>
            {c.section1.paragraphs.map(p => (
              <p class="section-paragraph" set:html={p.replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')} />
            ))}
          </section>

          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-heading">{c.section2.heading}</h2>
            {c.section2.paragraphs.map(p => (
              <p class="section-paragraph" set:html={p.replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')} />
            ))}
          </section>

          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-heading">{c.section3.heading}</h2>
            {c.section3.paragraphs.map(p => (
              <p class="section-paragraph" set:html={p.replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')} />
            ))}

            <div class="code-box">
              <div class="code-header">
                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                <span class="code-title">{c.section3.codeLabel}</span>
              </div>
              <pre class="code-block"><code>{c.section3.code}</code></pre>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="content-section conclusion-box">
            <h2 class="section-heading">{c.section4.heading}</h2>
            {c.section4.paragraphs.map(p => (
              <p class="section-paragraph" set:html={p.replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')} />
            ))}
          </section>

          <section class="comments-wrapper">
            <CommentsSection client:load postSlug="blog-cloud-run" currentLang={lang} />
          </section>
        </div>
      </article>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .blog-post-page {
    min-height: 100vh;
    background: radial-gradient(circle at top center, rgba(148, 163, 184, 0.1) 0%, rgba(15, 15, 26, 0.98) 70%);
    color: #e2e8f0;
    padding-bottom: 5rem;
  }

  .container {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: #38bdf8;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    transition: all 0.2s ease;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: #0284c7;
    transform: translateX(-4px);
  }

  .post-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .meta-badge {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.4rem 1rem;
    background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.12);
    border: 1px solid rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.25);
    border-radius: 9999px;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: #38bdf8;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .post-title {
    font-size: 2.25rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.25;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ffffff 0%, #cbd5e1 100%);
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .post-subtitle {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: #94a3b8;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .post-date {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: #cbd5e1;
  }

  .post-card {
    background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7);
    backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    border-radius: 1.5rem;
    padding: 2.5rem;
    box-shadow: 0 20px 40px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
  }

  .intro-bubble {
    background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.08);
    border-left: 4px solid #38bdf8;
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .intro-main {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #f8fafc;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .intro-desc {
    color: #cbd5e1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .extra-bullets {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .bullet-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: #e2e8f0;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .bullet-icon {
    color: #38bdf8;
  }

  .divider {
    border: 0;
    height: 1px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.3), transparent);
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  .content-section {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .section-heading {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #f8fafc;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .section-paragraph {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #cbd5e1;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  :global(.inline-blog-link) {
    color: #38bdf8;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    font-weight: 500;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }
  :global(.inline-blog-link:hover) {
    color: #7dd3fc;
  }

  .code-box {
    background: #0f172a;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .code-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 1rem;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
  }

  .code-dot {
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .code-dot.red { background: #ef4444; }
  .code-dot.yellow { background: #eab308; }
  .code-dot.green { background: #22c55e; }

  .code-title {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-family: monospace;
    color: #94a3b8;
    margin-left: 0.5rem;
  }

  .code-block {
    padding: 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: 'Fira Code', 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #38bdf8;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .conclusion-box {
    background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.05);
    border: 1px solid rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.2);
    border-radius: 1rem;
    padding: 1.75rem;
  }

  .comments-wrapper {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .post-title {
      font-size: 1.75rem;
    }
    .post-card {
      padding: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
